tool_args.new_string:
We showed that learned post-hoc correction of frozen flow-matching VLAs is harmful by default. Under a K-matched controlled comparison, unconditional correction degrades all four LIBERO suites on SmolVLA (0.4B) and all four suites on $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B; preliminary single-seed), with the largest degradation on Long where the CRM is out-of-distribution. The root cause is that the vast majority of control steps are already confident; corrections on these steps inject noise. Calibrated gating via $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distances detects confident steps and gates the CRM, recovering nearly all degradation.

The trigger rate---the fraction of steps where the gate fires---correlates monotonically with effect size across suites, providing a built-in diagnostic: practitioners can inspect the trigger rate on a new task to predict whether calibrated correction will yield positive gains or merely preserve baseline performance. Because $K{=}2$ batched inference matches $K{=}1$ latency via shared prefix encoding, the uncertainty probe is computationally free; the CRM adds only ${\sim}$0.25\,ms per triggered step, making the pipeline deployable without latency regression.

This is a diagnostic contribution: calibrated gating makes learned correction modules safe to deploy; without one, VLA-only remains the simpler choice. The key finding is that \emph{knowing when not to correct} matters more than correction strength. We expect this principle to extend beyond the specific CRM architecture tested here: any post-hoc correction module applied to a stochastic VLA faces the same confident-step noise injection problem, and calibrated gating provides a general-purpose safeguard.


\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Statistical power.}
Paired improvements ($\tau$-gated vs.\ VLA-only) reach statistical significance on Spatial ($p{=}0.017$, 8 seeds; Appendix~\ref{sec:8seed}) but not on other suites at $n{=}3$ seeds. The primary comparison (CRM $\tau$-gated vs.\ CRM always-on) is significant on all suites due to larger effect sizes, but the smaller $\tau$-gated vs.\ VLA-only improvements require more seeds to confirm.

\paragraph{Architecture scope.}
The finding applies to the tested MLP-based CRM (${\sim}$327K parameters); larger or more expressive correction architectures---such as attention-based or diffusion-based correctors---may exhibit different failure modes or benefit less from gating. The CRM's limited capacity may itself contribute to the noise injection on confident steps; whether a higher-capacity corrector can learn to self-gate remains an open question.

\paragraph{Stochastic diversity requirement.}
The $K{=}2$ pairwise distance probe requires diverse stochastic samples; deterministic VLAs (e.g., OpenVLA-OFT) produce near-identical outputs across forward passes, yielding no usable uncertainty signal (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}). This limits applicability to flow-matching or diffusion-based VLAs.

\paragraph{Threshold and generalization.}
The threshold $\tau$ is suite-dependent (Appendix~\ref{sec:tau_sweep}), and recovery on $\pi_{0.5}$ is partial (Table~\ref{tab:pi05_multiseed}). All experiments use LIBERO simulation with scripted demonstrations; real-robot transfer, adaptive $\tau$ selection, human-collected demonstrations, and evaluation on additional benchmarks remain future work.

